PART 2  DOES THE MEASURED ERROR MOVE WITH reps?   TIER A - real circuits, shots
==================================================================================================
  Same probes on every row (probe_seed=0), so only the device synthesis and
  the shot draw differ. 3 seeded simulator runs per row; +- is the sd.

   reps   circuits   mean rel err vs c_true     vs c_eff(reps)     depth
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      1       24       0.0682 +- 0.0125         0.0682 +- 0.0127         70
      2       24       0.0673 +- 0.0106         0.0673 +- 0.0106        140
      4       24       0.0677 +- 0.0086         0.0677 +- 0.0086        280
      8       24       0.0672 +- 0.0092         0.0672 +- 0.0092        560
     12       24       0.0672 +- 0.0095         0.0672 +- 0.0095        840
     24       24       0.0672 +- 0.0095         0.0672 +- 0.0095       1680

==================================================================================================
READING IT
==================================================================================================
  shipped default reps=12 :  0.0672 +- 0.0095  vs c_true
  cheapest        reps=1  :  0.0682 +- 0.0125
  most converged  reps=24 :  0.0672 +- 0.0095
  spread across reps      :  0.0010
  typical seed-to-seed sd :  0.0100

  FLAT within seed noise. The reported error does NOT come from the device
  model, so twirl_cal's attribution to first-order truncation in T stands,
  and the 3.0% headline is the estimator's own. device_reps is then free to
  lower for simulation speed.

  Scope: one coefficient draw (seed 7), N=3, noiseless, one T. Widening any of
  those is a separate experiment; this one asks only whether reps is a confound.

==================================================================================================
PART 3  THE SEED SPREAD, AND WHAT IT DOES TO THE REPORTED OPERATING POINT
==================================================================================================
  PART 2 measures 0.067 +- 0.010 at exactly the configuration twirl_cal reports
  0.0297 for. Reproducing that configuration directly over 4 unseeded trials
  gives 0.0662 +- 0.0246. The commit discloses 'no seed averaging on the circuit
  numbers'; this is what that disclosure costs. The estimator divides by
  <i[P_k,O]>, so terms a probe barely sees amplify shot noise, and the
  run-to-run spread is a large fraction of the mean.

  The reported table then rests on differences smaller than its own noise:
  0.0297 at 65536 shots against 0.0331 at 524288 was read as 'no gain,
  bias-limited'. Re-run with seeds, does that survive?

      T      shots    circuits    mean rel err (3 seeds)      max rel err
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    0.10      65536       24       0.2158 +- 0.0317            0.7908
    0.10     524288       24       0.0435 +- 0.0080            0.1651

    0.25      65536       24       0.0672 +- 0.0095            0.2716
    0.25     524288       24       0.0194 +- 0.0015            0.0693

    0.50      65536       24       0.0739 +- 0.0150            0.2050
    0.50     524288       24       0.0479 +- 0.0048            0.0866

    1.00      65536       24       0.1963 +- 0.0108            0.3084
    1.00     524288       24       0.1974 +- 0.0019            0.3066

==================================================================================================
READING PART 3
==================================================================================================
  (a) THE OPERATING POINT. Best T is 0.25 at 65536 shots and 0.25 at 524288 shots.
      At 65536 shots T=0.25 and T=0.50 differ by 0.0067 against sd 0.0150 - INSIDE
      the noise, so the low-shot row does not order them at all. The
      operating point is only resolvable at the higher shot count.

  (b) BIAS-LIMITED, OR SHOT-LIMITED? twirl_cal reads 0.0297 -> 0.0331 across
      8x shots as 'no gain - bias-limited'. Per T, the seed-averaged ratio:

        T      65536 shots      524288 shots     ratio    verdict
      --------------------------------------------------------------------------
       0.10     0.2158        0.0435        4.96x   shot-limited
       0.25     0.0672        0.0194        3.46x   shot-limited
       0.50     0.0739        0.0479        1.54x   bias-limited
       1.00     0.1963        0.1974        0.99x   bias-limited

      At the operating point T=0.25 the error falls 3.5x for 8x shots.
      THE 'BIAS-LIMITED' READING IS REFUTED THERE. It is shot-limited, and
      the original conclusion came from comparing two single draws whose
      difference (0.0034) is an eighth of the seed spread (~0.025). The
      diagnosis IS right at large T, where truncation dominates - which is
      why it looked plausible.

  (c) THE HEADLINE. 3.0% was a favourable single draw from a distribution
      centred near 6.6% at that shot count. Restated as a seed mean it is
      6.7% at 65536 shots and 1.9% at 524288 - and the second number is
      BETTER than the figure originally claimed, reached honestly. The
      comparison against QLTO fit()'s ~30% in 160 circuits survives either
      way, which is why restating costs nothing.
